00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
mercy pereira is struggling to keep the  home fires burning  for 35 years she has been making and  selling stringhoppers a popular sri  lankan staple  but with food prices skyrocketing  cooking gas and kerosene unavailable  she's struggling  i have to look after eight people i  manage all the expenses with what i make  even the water and electricity bills  if costs increase more than this i just  can't go on  mercy is not alone more sri lankans are  reeling as food inflation has raced past  50 percent  i found some items  about  three times more than even last week  yeah so it's always every day it's going  up  the person who bought kilos before now  buys 250 grams  things are at rock bottom  the united nations says the country's  economic crisis has affected food  security agriculture livelihoods and  access to health services  its world food program says three out of  four families are badly affected they're  cutting back on meals they're cutting  back on the portions of those meals and  they're buying food that's much cheaper  but much less nutritious the un and  non-government organizations are  appealing for almost 50 million dollars  for what they describe as life-saving  assistance to the worst-hit people but  there are bigger concerns  my fear is that there is a much larger  portion of the population who will very  soon need the same kind of assistance  unless there's a fundamental change in  the situation here  there's little sign of that at present  fuel supplies are running low  thousands of sri lankans queue for hours  sometimes days  energy minister kanchana vijay sakura  says he has no choice but to introduce a  weekly quarter for registered consumers  many people waiting in queues told al  jazeera they don't believe this will  work  another group fed up with shortages took  to the streets banging pots and pans and  kitchen utensils demanding president  gotaber rajapaksa and his latest  government step down  the government is trying desperately to  find a solution to the crisis but must  meet stringent conditions before it can  secure a bailout these don't mean much  for the tens of thousands of people  struggling to put food on the table mina  fernandez al-jazeera colombo
 

